// The bootstrap interpreter runs every worker on one host thread, so a
// blocking call blocks every worker (spec/README.md, Conformance: scheduling
// is implementation-defined). So probe 83_blocking_call_blocks_one_worker
// does not bind the bootstrap, and this probe checks the bootstrap's own
// choice in its place: a blocking call on a worker also blocks the main task.
//
// The worker wakes the main task with a send, then sleeps. Under the
// one-thread form the main task cannot run before the sleep ends, so the
// first receive returns only after the sleep. An implementation that blocks
// only the worker of the call returns from the receive at once.
fun sleeper(main) {
    task.send(main, "awake");
    sleep(1);
    task.send(main, "slept");
}

var start = time();
task.spawn_worker(sleeper, task.self());
print task.receive();
print time() - start >= 0.5;
print task.receive();

// STATUS: ok
// CHECK: awake
// CHECK: true
// CHECK: slept
